  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a complex but consistent relationship between (22R,25R)-spirosolan-3beta-ol and Carboxylesterase 2, mediated through multiple intermediate targets. The drug is directly linked to 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14, which in turn is targeted by several compounds, including (1R,3R,4S,7S,9S,11S,13R,14R,15R)-4,11,13,14-tetrahydroxy-7-[(2Z,4R)-4-(hydroxymethyl)hex-2-en-2-yl]-3,9,15-trimethoxy-12,12-dimethyl-6,17-dioxabicyclo[11.3.1]heptadecan-5-one and (2R,3S,4R,5R,6S)-2-[2-(3,4-dihydroxy-5-methoxyphenyl)-7-hydroxy-3-[(2R,3S,4R,5R,6S)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxychromenylium-5-yl]oxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxane-3,4,5-triol. These compounds are all targets of Carboxylesterase 2, indicating that they are metabolized by this enzyme. Additionally, (22R,25R)-spirosolan-3beta-ol is also linked to Dehydrogenase/reductase SDR family member 9, which targets Flavipin, which in turn targets Carboxylesterase 2. While the exact mechanism of interaction between (22R,25R)-spirosolan-3beta-ol and Carboxylesterase 2 remains unclear, the evidence strongly suggests that the drug is metabolized by this enzyme, likely through a series of intermediate targets that are themselves substrates of the enzyme. This pathway highlights the enzyme's role in the metabolism of multiple compounds, emphasizing its importance in drug action and metabolic pathways.",
